      "writtenOn": "2020-07-06T23:19:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay so the code basically looks for a {package,overview}.html file to see if it contains @hide, and if it does, hide the entire package. The problem is these files don\u0027t contain java package names, so metalava tries to infer it from *.java files in the same directory.\n\nFor example, for http://cs/android/frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/package.html, which contains @hide, metalava first assumes the package name is `services.core.java.com.android.server.am`, which is the relative path from the base path (which happens to be wrong) so it looks at ContentProviderRecord.java and get the right package from it. If there are no java files in the directory, metalava goes with the wrong guess and hide `services.core.java.com.android.server.am` instead of the correct package `com.android.server.am`... Which happens to not matter because this directory is hidden by other means too (not sure how exactly, but it must be working this way).\n\nThe javadoc says File.listFiles()\u0027s resulting order has no guarantees (https://docs.oracle.com/javase/7/docs/api/java/io/File.html#listFiles()), so whitelisting these don\u0027t even work...\n\n\nThere\u0027s actually another way to hide a package using `package-info.java` (e.g. http://cs/android/frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/package-info.java), but looks like metalava also keeps the content of {package,overview}.html files for later use, so we can\u0027t just migrate to package-info.java...\n\n\nSo I think we need to give another way for metalava to get the package name from these html files.\n\n\nI think we could use a new @ tag, like this?  `@javapackage com.android.server.am`\n\nIf adding a new @tag is problematic, we could put it in a comment: `\u003c!-- @java-package com.android.server.am --\u003e`",
      "parentUuid": "f1305f65_c4ca459b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 51,
        "startChar": 42,
        "endLine": 57,
        "endChar": 48
      },
